# Chroma library, modern Earth: CLOSE PEOPLE'S WANTS AND THE GROUPS THAT GATE MOMENTS. Library thread, 2026-10-09,
# "v22" update stage 3, items C1 and C2 (chroma-world/model/stage3-rules.md section 5).
# C1: six everyday moments that answer a close person's want (cast_want:, the holder in the first who: slot), two more
# each for care, stop and grandchild beside the one each in earth-world-people.lib, so each want has three. Every one
# offers a yes, a no and a middle way. Which options meet the want follows the Engine's own reading (world_link.py: a
# mark of helping or coming home, a title, a grant, binds, or benevolence among the values; a refusal mark never does),
# noted above each moment as "meets:". Three everyday pairs (two colors called / the other three, matching gates);
# cycles s2 2, s4 2, s1 1, s3 1.
# C2: three group moments as life events (group: gates them to members, and per_year is the yearly rate among members,
# so the gate does not thin it out): a congregation splits (D1a pairs), the gang's test (D1b), the club's final (D2a).
# Chances: each moment is even on its own (everyday: one-color chance plus its cross option's chance is 140 for every
# color; life events: one-color chance plus half its two pair options' chances is the same for every color).
# Syntax: header of build.py.

# ================================================================ C1: close people's wants

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 1: care, calls WB (cycle s2) / stop, calls URG (cycle s4)

# meets: W, U, G, W>B, R>W, G>U (yes or a middle way that gives care); not: B, U>R, B>G (care handed to others), R (no)
== a grandparent who forgets the way home
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult adult
age: 18-45
calls: WB
rate: .5
tone: trouble
life: family, health, home
horizon: months
roles: grandparent, parent, sibling, a doctor
who: grandparent
cast_want: care
earth: a grandparent's memory is failing, and someone in the family has to step in for the months ahead
times: About 1 in 10 people over 65 lives with memory loss, more after 80 (estimate leaning on Alzheimer's Disease International); grandchildren give some of the care in perhaps 1 family in 6 where it comes, most often when the parents work full time (estimate)
gap: 3-10
share: .12
scene: {grandparent} was found at the bus station at dawn, sure they were late for a job they left forty years ago. {parent} works long shifts, and at the family meeting everyone turns to look at {N}.
scene W: {grandparent} sat up with {N} through every childhood fever. A debt like that does not go away because it is inconvenient.
scene U: {N} has read about this illness: how fast it moves, what the first year asks of a family, and which days are the hard ones.
scene B: Someone has to carry this, and {N} has a new job, a lease and a life just starting. {N} means to see it done well without losing all of that.
scene R: {grandparent} looks at {N} across the kitchen and, for one terrible second, does not know who {N} is.
scene G: {grandparent} taught {N} to plant beans and read the weather. The old allotment is still there, gone wild, at the end of the lane.
- W | move in with them for the months it takes, and keep their days in order | v: benevolence, tradition | mark: helped someone in need | tags: pay:money-.03 | chance: 60
- U | read up on the illness, and take them to the memory clinic yourself | v: benevolence, self-direction | chance: 75
- B | find them a good care home, and sell their flat to pay for it | v: security | chance: 65
- R | tell the family you have your own life to start, and leave it to them | v: self-direction | mark: refused someone in need | chance: 85
- G | take them to the old allotment every Sunday, and work the ground beside them | binds | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 80
- W>B | draw up a fair rota with the family, take your share, and hold everyone to theirs | v: benevolence, power | chance: 80
- U>R | fit door alarms and a tracker, so they can stay home and you keep working | v: security, self-direction | tech: phone | act: fit door alarms and a tracker, so they can stay home and the job is kept | chance: 65
- B>G | pay a neighbour to look in on them twice a day | v: security | tags: pay:money-.04 | chance: 75
- R>W | take three months off work from Monday, and care for them yourself | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | tags: pay:money-.05 | act: take three months off work from Monday, and care for them in person | chance: 55
- G>U | go through the old photograph albums with them every evening, and keep the memories alive | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 60
works: {grandparent} settles into the new routine, and the frightening nights stop.
works: The months are hard, but {grandparent} has good days again, and {N} is there for most of them.
fails: Within weeks the arrangement is fraying, and {N} is running on four hours' sleep.
fails: {grandparent} goes missing again one afternoon, and the whole question opens from the start.

# meets: W, U>W, B>U, R>B, G>R (the friend dropped, or kept only on the parent's terms); not: U, G, W>G (a middle way of
# their own), B, R (no)
== a parent asks you to drop a friend
tier: everyday
stages: juvenile young_adult
age: 16-28
calls: URG
rate: .5
tone: trouble
life: family, friends
horizon: months
roles: parent, friend, sibling
who: parent
cast_want: stop
earth: a parent begs you to stop running around with a friend who keeps landing in trouble with the police
times: Many parents of teenagers and young adults worry about one friend in particular, and perhaps 1 in 4 asks outright for the friendship to end at some point (estimate); peers are the strongest single predictor of youth offending (Warr 2002)
gap: 2-6
share: .2
scene: {friend} was stopped by the police again on Saturday night, and this time {N} was in the car. {parent} has waited up at the kitchen table, and says it plainly: {N} has to stop seeing {friend}.
scene W: {N} gave {parent} a promise last year to stay out of trouble. Saturday night broke it, whatever {friend} says about whose fault it was.
scene U: {N} knows more about {friend} than {parent} does: the good side, the bad side, and exactly where the trouble usually starts.
scene B: {friend} knows people, gets into places, and has never once let {N} pay for anything. Giving that up has a price.
scene R: {friend} is the most alive person {N} knows, and nobody, not even {parent}, gets to choose {Ns} friends.
scene G: {friend} and {N} have known each other since the first day of school. Their families used to share a garden fence.
- W | give your word to stop seeing that friend, and keep it | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: kept your word | act: give their word to stop seeing that friend, and keep it | chance: 65
- U | ask what exactly frightens them, and weigh it against what you know of your friend | v: self-direction | chance: 85
- B | keep the friend, and simply stop telling your parent where you go | v: self-direction, hedonism | mark: hid a wrong | chance: 70
- R | tell them your friends are your own business, and slam the door | v: self-direction | mark: defied an authority | chance: 90
- G | see the friend only at the old places, the park and the café | v: tradition, security | chance: 75
- W>G | bring the friend home for dinner, so your parent can see who they really are | v: tradition, universalism | chance: 75
- U>W | agree a clear rule with your parent: no rides in that car, home by eleven | v: security, benevolence | chance: 55
- B>U | trade it: drop the friend, if your parent lends you the car at weekends | binds | v: power, self-direction | chance: 70
- R>B | cut the friend off with one blunt message tonight, and block the number | v: security, benevolence | tech: phone | chance: 50
- G>R | go out with the friend one last time, like the old days, then say goodbye | v: hedonism, benevolence | chance: 65
works: The talk at the kitchen table clears the air, and the nights of waiting up are over.
works: Whatever {N} chose holds through the months that follow, and {parent} begins to trust it.
fails: Within a month there is another late call from {friend}, and the row starts again.
fails: {parent} and {N} stop speaking about it, and then stop speaking much at all.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 2: care, calls UB (cycle s1) / stop, calls WRG (cycle s3); both need a partner

# meets: W, U, G, W>U, U>B, R>G; not: B, G>W (care handed to others), B>R, R (no)
== a partner's long months of treatment
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 25-90
calls: UB
needs: partner
rate: .5
tone: trouble
life: family, health, love
horizon: months
roles: partner, boss, friend, a nurse
who: partner
cast_want: care
earth: your partner faces six months of hard treatment, and the days ahead will need you
times: About 1 adult in 3 cares for an ill partner at some point, most of them after 60 (estimate); serious illness with months of treatment reaches about half of all couples who grow old together (estimate)
gap: 5-15
share: .3
scene: {partner} starts treatment on Monday: six months of hospital mornings, sick afternoons and long nights. The nurse's leaflet lists everything {partner} will not be able to do alone, and the list is long.
scene W: {N} promised long ago to stand by {partner} through anything. Those were easy words then. They are not easy now.
scene U: {N} has the treatment plan open on the table: dates, doses, side effects, and the days when {partner} must not be left alone.
scene B: The mortgage does not stop for illness, and neither does {boss}. {N} is working out how to keep the job and still be there.
scene R: {partner} tries to make a joke about losing their hair, and {N} has to leave the room so as not to cry in front of them.
scene G: {Ns} grandparents went through something like this, and {Ns} grandmother nursed her husband at home to the end, the way people did.
- W | take leave from work, and be at their side for every treatment | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: helped someone in need | tags: pay:money-.05 | chance: 60
- U | learn the treatment plan inside out, and run the medicines and the diary | binds | v: benevolence, achievement | chance: 70
- B | keep working full time, and pay a carer to cover the days | v: security, achievement | tags: pay:money-.05 | chance: 75
- R | admit you cannot face the hospital, and leave the treatment days to their family | v: self-direction | mark: refused someone in need | chance: 85
- G | cook their favourite meals, and sit with them through the bad evenings | v: benevolence | chance: 80
- W>U | set up a written rota of friends and family for every treatment day | v: security, benevolence | chance: 80
- U>B | work out shorter hours with your boss, and take the cut in pay | v: benevolence, security | tags: pay:money-.04 | chance: 70
- B>R | keep your evenings out with friends, and let the nurses do the caring | v: hedonism, self-direction | mark: refused someone in need | chance: 65
- R>G | drop everything and drive them to every appointment, whatever work says | v: benevolence | chance: 55
- G>W | ask their brothers and sisters to share the care, as families used to | v: tradition | chance: 60
works: The treatment ends, and {partner} rings the bell on the ward with {N} beside them.
works: The months are long, but the care holds, and the two of them come out of it closer.
fails: Halfway through, {N} is worn so thin that the smallest thing starts a row.
fails: The arrangement breaks down in the second month, and {partner} feels like a burden.

# meets: W, G, W>R, U>G (the dangerous work given up, now or by a date); not: U, B, B>W, G>B (a middle way that keeps
# the work), R, R>U (no)
== a partner asks you to quit the dangerous work
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 20-60
calls: WRG
needs: partner
holds: kind:career
rate: .5
tone: trouble
life: family, work, love
horizon: months
roles: partner, boss, colleague, friend
who: partner
cast_want: stop
earth: after a near miss at work, your partner asks you to give up the dangerous job for good
times: About 1 worker in 12 does a job with a high risk of serious injury: building, roofing, fishing, mining, forestry, long-distance driving (estimate leaning on labour statistics of fatal injury by trade); a near miss or a colleague's accident often brings the question home (estimate)
gap: 3-10
share: .08
scene: The scaffold gave way two metres from where {N} stood. Nobody was hurt this time, but {partner} heard about it on the local news before {N} could call. Tonight {partner} says it quietly: please find other work.
scene W: {N} signed up for this work knowing the rules, and keeps to every one of them. But {partner} did not sign up for anything.
scene U: {N} knows the figures for this trade better than most: the injuries a year, the commonest causes, and which of them a careful worker can avoid.
scene B: The danger money pays the mortgage. No safer job in town pays half as well, and {N} knows it.
scene R: Up there, at the top of the scaffold with the whole town below, {N} has never felt more alive. That is the part {partner} will never understand.
scene G: {Ns} grandfather did this work, and his father before him. The crew is like a second family.
- W | give a month's notice, and look for safer work | v: security, benevolence | chance: 65
- U | study the accident report, and show them the real odds before deciding | v: self-direction, security | chance: 85
- B | stay on for the danger money, only until the mortgage is paid | v: power, security | chance: 70
- R | tell them this work is who you are, and you are not giving it up | v: self-direction, stimulation | act: tell them this work is who they are, and they are not giving it up | chance: 80
- G | take the safer ground-level work the firm offered, at lower pay | v: security, benevolence | tags: pay:money-.03 | chance: 70
- W>R | promise to quit within the year, and mark the date on the calendar together | v: benevolence, security | mark: kept your word | chance: 75
- U>G | retrain at night school for a trade with both feet on the ground | v: security, benevolence | mark: learned a skill | chance: 55
- B>W | demand the firm fix its safety rules, or you walk out | v: security, power | lever: voice | pushes: institution employer | act: demand the firm fix its safety rules, or they walk out | chance: 70
- R>U | go back up the very next morning, to prove you can still do it | v: stimulation, achievement | mark: took a wild risk | act: go back up the very next morning, to prove they can still do it | chance: 60
- G>B | ask the oldest hand on the crew how they lasted forty years up there | v: tradition, security | chance: 70
works: The decision is made, and for the first time in months {partner} sleeps through the night.
works: {N} and {partner} come out of the talk with a plan both of them can live with.
fails: The question hangs between them for months, and every late shift starts a fresh argument.
fails: A colleague is hurt on the site that winter, and the whole fight opens again.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 3: grandchild, calls BG (cycle s4) / calls WUR (cycle s2); both exclude children

# meets: W, R, W>G (a child hoped for and welcomed); not: U, U>W, G, G>R (a middle way), B, B>U, R>B (no)
== a small blanket in a parent's knitting bag
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult adult
age: 25-45
calls: BG
excludes: children
rate: .5
tone: mixed
life: family, children
horizon: years
roles: parent, partner, sibling
who: parent
cast_want: grandchild
earth: you find a parent quietly knitting a baby blanket, for one day, with no hurry at all
times: Most parents in their sixties hope for grandchildren, and many say so only by hints (estimate); about 1 in 5 adults in rich countries reaches 45 with no children (OECD Family Database, estimate)
gap: 3-8
share: .25
scene: Looking for scissors, {N} finds it in {parent}'s knitting bag: a small soft blanket, half done. {parent} blushes and says it is for one day, no hurry, and nothing more is said.
scene W: A family goes on through its children. {N} has always known it was expected, the way a duty is expected, and the blanket says it without a word.
scene U: {N} turns the blanket over. The stitches are months old at the start and fresh at the end: {parent} has been working on this for a long time.
scene B: {Ns} life is built just as {N} wants it: the work, the flat, the plans for the next five years. None of it was drawn up around a child.
scene R: The tiny blanket in {Ns} hands hits harder than any question at the dinner table ever did.
scene G: {parent} knitted one just like this for {N}, once. It is still in a drawer somewhere, soft with age.
- W | tell them you hope for children one day, and the blanket will be used | v: benevolence, tradition | act: tell them they hope for children one day, and the blanket will be used | chance: 65
- U | ask them what a grandchild would mean to them, and listen to the answer | v: self-direction, universalism | chance: 85
- B | say plainly that children are not in your plans, and ask them to stop knitting | v: self-direction | chance: 70
- R | laugh, hug them, and ask for stripes on it | v: hedonism, benevolence | chance: 80
- G | put the blanket back gently, and say a child will come if it is meant to | v: tradition | chance: 85
- W>G | promise them they will be part of any family you ever raise | binds | v: benevolence, tradition | act: promise them they will be part of any family they ever raise | chance: 75
- U>W | explain honestly where you stand, and what would have to change for a child | v: self-direction, conformity | act: explain honestly where they stand, and what would have to change for a child | chance: 55
- B>U | suggest they finish it for the cousin who is expecting, and leave your life to you | v: self-direction, power | act: suggest they finish it for the cousin who is expecting, and leave their life to them | chance: 70
- R>B | tell them sharply that your life is not a family project | v: self-direction, power | chance: 60
- G>R | take them out for the day, and show them the life you have instead | v: stimulation, tradition | chance: 55
works: The blanket goes back in the bag, and something between {N} and {parent} is easier for it.
works: {parent} hugs {N} at the door and says, just this once, that whatever comes is all right.
fails: The blanket sits between them at every visit for a year, unspoken.
fails: {parent} is hurt, and the next few phone calls are short and careful.

# meets: W, G, R>W, G>U (a child hoped for, or another way to a family); not: U, W>B, B, B>G (a middle way), R, U>R (no)
== a parent's one wish on a big birthday
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult adult
age: 25-45
calls: WUR
excludes: children
rate: .5
tone: mixed
life: family, children
horizon: years
roles: parent, partner, sibling, the aunts and uncles
who: parent
cast_want: grandchild
earth: at a parent's seventieth birthday, asked for a wish, they say they would love to hold a grandchild one day
times: A parent's big birthday comes every ten years; perhaps 1 in 3 parents of adults with no children names a grandchild as their wish at some point, kindly and more than once (estimate)
gap: 3-10
share: .2
scene: The candles are lit and the whole table shouts for a wish. {parent} looks across at {N}, smiles, and says the only wish left is to hold a grandchild one day. Then, quickly: no pressure, none at all.
scene W: Every aunt and uncle at the table is watching {N}. In this family, a parent's wish on a birthday has always been treated as something close to sacred.
scene U: {N} notices the timing: the wine, the candles, all of them listening. {parent} is not a planner, but this was not quite an accident either.
scene B: A child is a twenty-year commitment of money, time and freedom. {N} will not sign up for that because of a birthday cake.
scene R: {Ns} face goes hot. It is a lovely wish and an awful moment, and {N} wants to laugh and leave the room at the same time.
scene G: Seventy years. The photographs on the wall run back four generations, and {parent} is the oldest one left alive.
- W | raise a glass and promise them the wish will come true | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 60
- U | take a week to think it over, then give them an honest answer | v: self-direction | chance: 90
- B | smile, say nothing, and change the subject to the cake | v: security, conformity | chance: 85
- R | tell them, there and then, that it is a wish you cannot grant | v: self-direction | act: tell them, there and then, that it is a wish they cannot grant | chance: 60
- G | hug them and say life will bring what it brings | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 80
- W>B | tell them you have set a time to decide, and keep to it | v: security, conformity | chance: 80
- U>R | explain calmly why you are choosing a life without children, and ask them to respect it | v: self-direction, universalism | act: explain calmly why they are choosing a life without children, and ask for respect | chance: 50
- B>G | say there may be a child in a few years, if the timing and money allow | v: security, tradition | chance: 55
- R>W | tell them, moved, that you mean to start a family soon | binds | v: benevolence, tradition | act: tell them, moved, that they mean to start a family soon | chance: 80
- G>U | tell them you may adopt a child one day, and ask what they think | v: benevolence, universalism | act: tell them they may adopt a child one day, and ask what they think | chance: 60
works: The candles are blown out to cheers, and {parent} squeezes {Ns} hand under the table.
works: Whatever {N} said is taken kindly, and the wish is not raised again that year.
fails: The table goes quiet, and the rest of the party has a strained, careful edge.
fails: {parent} is hurt, and the aunts talk about it on the phone for weeks.

# ================================================================ C2: groups that gate moments (life events, rates among members)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: a congregation splits (pairs D1a)

# world: revival, selector members, share .5
# world: movement founded, selector members, share .5
# world: congregation drift above .10 from its faith body, selector members, share .3
== the congregation splits in two
tier: life event
group: congregation
at: faith body
stages: juvenile+
age: 14-100
per_year: 0 .007 .007 .007 .007 .007
drivers: unrest+.3 stress+.2 community+.2
gap: 10-30
times: Among members of a congregation only (group: congregation; the rate is the yearly rate among members): serious conflict is common, with about 1 congregation in 4 reporting a conflict that made some people leave within two years (estimate leaning on the National Congregations Study), and a full split into two bodies is a small part of that, set at 7 in 1,000 a year from 14 (about 1 lifelong member in 3 sees one, estimate). With the world on, the Engine also fires it for the members of that congregation (selector members) in a revival year, in the year a new movement is founded in the place, or when the congregation's norm has drifted far from its faith body's (tv above .10)
likelier: a revival or a new movement in the place, a leader who divides, a quarrel over teaching or money, a congregation drifting from its faith body, hard times
rarer: a small, settled congregation with a long-serving leader, secure times, a faith body that keeps its congregations close
tone: trouble
life: faith, community, family
horizon: months
roles: parent, grandparent, friend, sibling, a leader of the congregation
earth: the congregation you belong to splits in two, and half the families start meeting in a hall across town
scene: For a year the arguments ran on after every service. Now it is done: the leader and half the families will meet in a rented hall across town, and the rest will keep the old building. Everyone wants to know where {N} will sit next week.
scene W: The old building has its rules, its elders and its order of service, kept the same for a hundred years. {N} was taught that a congregation does not walk away from its own house.
scene U: {N} has listened to both sides for a year, and has noticed that neither side quite says what the quarrel is really about.
scene B: The building, the funds and the board will go to one half. Whoever ends up with them will run things for a generation.
scene R: The leader's voice still moves {N} the way it did the first time. The old elders have never once made {N} feel anything.
scene G: {grandparent} was married in the old building, and so was {parent}. The families who sat in those rows are being torn apart.
- W | stay in the old building with the elders, and keep the old order | v: tradition, conformity | lever: loyalty | pushes: group congregation | chance: 80
- U | go to both for a month, and see which keeps faith with its own teaching | v: self-direction, universalism | chance: 70
- B | side with whichever half keeps the building, the funds and the board | v: power, security | lever: loyalty | pushes: group congregation | chance: 75
- R | follow the leader to the new hall, whatever the elders say | v: stimulation, self-direction | lever: exit | pushes: group congregation | chance: 70
- G | stay with the families you grew up beside, wherever they go | v: tradition, benevolence | lever: loyalty | pushes: group congregation | chance: 70
- WU | call a meeting of both halves, and ask for a fair vote on the building | v: universalism, conformity | lever: voice | pushes: group congregation | chance: 45
- WR | stand up at the last shared service and say the split shames them all | v: tradition, universalism | mark: made an enemy | lever: voice | pushes: group congregation | chance: 55
- UG | stop going to either, and keep your faith at home for now | v: self-direction, tradition | lever: neglect | pushes: group congregation | chance: 75
- BR | take charge of the new hall's money and rota before anyone else does | v: power, achievement | pushes: group congregation | chance: 65
- BG | broker a quiet deal to share the old building on alternate weeks | v: security, benevolence | lever: voice | pushes: group congregation | chance: 45
works: A year on, {N} has a place where {N} belongs, and the worst of the bitterness has passed.
works: Some of the families find their way back to each other, and {N} helped make it happen.
fails: Old friends cross the road rather than speak, and {N} is caught in the middle of it.
fails: The half {N} chose falls apart within two years, and the hall stands empty.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: the gang's test (pairs D1b)

== the gang's test of loyalty
tier: life event
group: gang
stages: juvenile+
age: 16-60
per_year: 0 .12 .08 .04 .02 0
drivers: unrest+.3 harsh+.2 prosper-.2
gap: 2-8
times: Among members of a gang only (group: gang; the rate is the yearly rate among members): studies of street gangs describe tests of loyalty after joining as near universal, mostly in the first years (Decker and Van Winkle 1996, estimate), set at 12 in 100 a year at 16 and 17, 8 in 100 as a young adult, fewer later as members rise or drift out (estimate)
likelier: a new or doubted member, a gang under pressure from the police or a rival, a member with ties outside the gang
rarer: a long-trusted member, a member near the top, a gang winding down
tone: trouble
life: friends, crime, home
horizon: week
roles: friend, parent, sibling, the older members
earth: the gang tells you to prove your loyalty: keep a locked bag for a week, and say nothing, whoever asks
scene: {friend}, who brought {N} in, hands over a heavy sports bag with a padlock on the zip. Keep it for a week, ask nothing, and if anyone comes asking, {N} has never seen it. The older members are watching to see what {N} does.
scene W: Being part of something means doing what it asks. {N} gave a promise to these people, and the promise is being tested now.
scene U: {N} weighs the bag in one hand. Too heavy for clothes. Whatever is in it, the law will care about it more than the gang will.
scene B: Everyone who rose in this crew passed a test like this. {N} can see the next step up from here, and what it pays.
scene R: The thrill and the fear arrive together. {N} could say yes, or walk out the door, and both feel like jumping off something high.
scene G: {N} grew up on these streets with these people. {parent} is at home right now, cooking, with no idea any of this exists.
- W | keep the bag and your silence for the week, exactly as told | v: conformity, security | mark: broke the law | closed: law: hiding goods for a gang; backfire: the police search the house, and the bag is found | lever: loyalty | pushes: group gang | chance: 50
- U | open the bag in secret first, to know exactly what you are risking | v: self-direction, security | chance: 70
- B | keep the bag, and ask for a cut of whatever it is worth | v: power | mark: broke the law | closed: law: hiding goods for a gang; backfire: the police search the house, and the bag is found | lever: loyalty | pushes: group gang | chance: 70
- R | refuse to their faces, and walk out of the gang for good | v: self-direction | mark: made an enemy | lever: exit | pushes: group gang | chance: 55
- G | tell your family everything, and let them help you get out | v: security, tradition | act: tell their family everything, and let them help get them out | lever: exit | pushes: group gang | chance: 45
